2026-10-23  DMA version of the Keccak accelerator
=====================================================================

New files
---------
* source/sources_1/imports/src/keccak_accelerator_dma_wb.v: wraps the unchanged
  keccak_accelerator_wb and adds a DMA engine with a Wishbone master port. Commands:
  ABSORB (XOR from RAM, permute after each full rate block), SQUEEZE (permute + write a
  block to RAM, repeated), LOAD/STORE (copy state <-> RAM). Pipelined, 1 word per cycle;
  partial words via byte enables; rejected commands / bus errors set DONE + ERROR.
  Registers at 0x200-0x210, inner map unchanged (old firmware runs on it as is).
* source/sources_1/imports/src/barebones_wb_top_dma.v: copy of barebones_wb_top with the
  DMA accelerator. Memory port 1 is shared between the core data bus and the DMA master
  with fixed core priority, so the core's timing is unchanged (the core ignores ack and
  expects data one cycle after a request, so it must never be stalled by the DMA).
  barebones_wb_top.v is untouched.
* keccak_test/rtl/keccak_accelerator_dma_wb_tb.v + keccak_test/sw/dma_test.c: DMA unit
  test with a stalling RAM model (5 cases, see README). Outputs were also cross-checked
  once against Python hashlib (SHAKE256, SHAKE128, SHA3-256 shaped cases).

Changes
-------
* fips202.c / params.h: -DKECCAK_DMA driver path. Word-aligned buffers use DMA ABSORB /
  SQUEEZE, acc_load/acc_store use LOAD/STORE; unaligned buffers fall back to register
  access. Compiler "memory" barriers around each DMA command.
* Build options: `DMA=1` for cbr.sh, the firmware Makefile (-DKECCAK_DMA) and the
  Verilator Makefile (+define+KECCAK_DMA, separate obj_dir_dma).
  keccak_test: `make` runs both unit tests, `make test` / `make test_dma` run one.
* barebones_top_tb.v: selects barebones_wb_top_dma with +define+KECCAK_DMA; 100M-cycle
  timeout (TEST TIMEOUT), e.g. for a DMA firmware image on the non-DMA top.

Verification
------------
* keccak_test: both unit tests pass.
* SoC: default flow unchanged (identical cycle counts). DMA flow passes the FIPS 202 KATs
  and the Kyber cross-check. The non-DMA firmware on the DMA top passes with identical
  cycle counts.

Results (Kyber768, cycles)
--------------------------
                      software Keccak    accelerator    accelerator + DMA
  keypair generation      1,914,451          757,870        677,783 (2.82x)
  encapsulation           2,170,200          986,550        904,783 (2.40x)
  decapsulation           2,492,148        1,343,837      1,275,188 (1.95x)
  The remaining time is mostly polynomial arithmetic (NTT etc.), not Keccak.

Not done
--------
* Completion interrupt (still polling).
* Vivado synthesis/timing of the new RTL (Verilator only).


2026-10-22  Keccak accelerator rework, driver fixes and verification
=====================================================================

Bug fixes
---------
* test/ml-kem-kyber/fips202.c: KeccakF1600_StatePermute never read the permuted state back
  from the accelerator, so SHA3/SHAKE outputs were not FIPS 202. The ML-KEM self-test still
  passed because keypair/enc/dec all used the same (wrong) hash. The previous cycle report
  was therefore invalid.
* test/ml-kem-kyber/fips202.c: accelerator registers were accessed through non-volatile
  pointers; at -O2 GCC removed the `en = 0` store and could legally reorder the state
  stores past the start write. All MMIO now goes through volatile uint32_t accesses.
* test/ml-kem-kyber/test/Makefile: objcopy did not extract .data, so initialised globals
  (the randombytes seed `prng_state`) were zero on the SoC. `.data` is now part of the image.
* source/.../keccak_accelerator_wb.v: en/done/load/sbuf were driven from two always blocks
  (multi-driven nets in synthesis), round constants were `assign`ed to a reg array,
  wb_sel_i was ignored, and partial address decoding aliased the state buffer across the
  4 KiB window and returned garbage for 0x004-0x00C.

Accelerator (source/sources_1/imports/src/keccak_accelerator_wb.v) - rewritten
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* One full round (theta, rho, pi, chi, iota) per clock: 24 cycles per permutation
  (was 2 rounds per 19 cycles, ~230 cycles).
* Single 1600-bit state register, directly accessible over the bus (previously the state
  was held three times: sbuf, A* and E* registers).
* New XOR window at offset 0x120 for absorbing in place, CLEAR command (CTRL bit1),
  STATUS register with busy bit, sticky done cleared on start.
* Byte enables honoured, strict address decoding (unmapped reads return 0), registered
  read data, state writes ignored while busy, one driver per register.
* Register map stays backward compatible with the old EN (0x000) / DN (0x010) /
  ST (0x020) layout. See README.md for the full map.

Driver (test/ml-kem-kyber/fips202.c, params.h)
----------------------------------------------
* Accelerator driver with acc_load/acc_store/acc_xor_bytes/acc_read_bytes helpers.
* sha3_256, sha3_512, shake128, shake256 run entirely in the accelerator (absorb via XOR
  window, squeeze by reading the state), no state copies to RAM.
* keccak_absorb_once and keccak_squeezeblocks transfer the state at most once per call
  instead of once per permutation.
* Reference software permutation restored behind -DKECCAK_SW (was commented out).
* params.h: KECCAK_EN/DN/ST_ADDR replaced by KECCAK_BASE_ADDR + register offsets.

Verification
------------
* keccak_test: the unit test now simulates the SoC source file directly (the duplicated
  keccak_test/rtl/keccak_accelerator_wb.v was removed). It covers 7 cases: legacy flow,
  3 back-to-back permutations, clear + zero state, XOR-window absorb with byte-enable
  writes, and STATE/XOR/CTRL writes while busy. P(0) matches the published
  Keccak-f[1600] vector (F1258F7940E1DDE7...).
* hornet_kyber.c: FIPS 202 known-answer tests (SHA3-256/512, SHAKE128/256; empty, short,
  multi-block, rate-1/rate padding boundaries, multi-block squeeze, incremental API with
  odd chunk sizes). Expected values come from Python hashlib. Fails with debug code 'H'.
* hornet_kyber.c + new gen_kat.c: Kyber pk/sk/ct hashes and the shared secret are compared
  with a host run of the same code using the software Keccak (kyber_kat.h is generated by
  the Makefile). Fails with debug code 'K'.
* Negative check: putting the original no-readback bug back into the driver makes the
  firmware fail with 'H'.
* barebones_top_tb.v: prints the cycle number of every debug write and calls $finish on
  'P'/'F' (the simulation previously never terminated).

Results (Kyber768, same SoC and seed, see cycle_report.txt)
-----------------------------------------------------------
                      software Keccak    accelerator    speedup
  keypair generation      1,914,451          757,870      2.53x
  encapsulation           2,170,200          986,550      2.20x
  decapsulation           2,492,148        1,343,837      1.85x

Tooling
-------
* cbr.sh: `set -e`, runs from any directory, IS_MULTILIB / KECCAK_SW from the environment.
* verilator/Makefile: `clean` now removes obj_dir (rm -f -> rm -rf).
* keccak_test/Makefile: DUT taken from source/, --timescale for the timescale-less DUT.
* Regenerated memory images (memory_init_tb.mem) for the new firmware.

Not done (needs decisions / changes outside this scope)
-------------------------------------------------------
* Completion interrupt: needs an interconnect/top-level change to route it to fast_irq.
  Polling is cheap at 24 cycles.
* DMA: done in a separate top, see the 2026-10-23 entry above.
* Vivado synthesis/timing of the new RTL was not run here (Verilator only).
